WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.452 --> 00:00:02.410
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I came
out and I have to ask,

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:05.400
what exactly is going on?

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.900
MARTIN OMANDER:
Well, Katie, I think

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.820
I made the perfect chocolate
chip cookie recipe,

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.510
but I'm having issues with
portioning and assuring

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.008
each cookie will bake
and taste the same.

00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:17.550
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I mean, the
dough looks pretty good itself.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.050
I'd love to get the recipe.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.240
But as for portioning,
I have some tools here

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.250
that might be able to
help you get consistency

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.778
with each cookie.

00:00:24.778 --> 00:00:26.445
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that would be great.

00:00:26.445 --> 00:00:29.626
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.050
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I didn't know
you were an amateur baker as

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.572
well as being a software dev.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:41.780
MARTIN OMANDER: That's right.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.870
When my code is done, I turn
it over to the customer.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.540
They then deploy it in
their cloud project,

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.870
according to a long list
instructions that I've written.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.930
But the problem is that
these deployments often

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.220
turn out like my cookies--

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.390
uneven and not very good.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.835
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: That
does sound frustrating.

00:01:00.835 --> 00:01:01.710
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.010
It makes the customer unhappy
and makes me look bad.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.580
I know my code is correct, but
if it's deployed incorrectly,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.218
it won't work.

00:01:09.218 --> 00:01:11.010
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Have
you considered using

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.630
an infrastructure as code tool?

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.750
Instead of your documentation,
you could write it all as code.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.400
Then you get
repeatable deployments

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.280
in a machine-readable language.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.210
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, sounds great.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.290
What's the tool?

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:25.790
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
There are several.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.670
But one that's widely used
right now is called Terraform.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.930
It supports many
major cloud platforms,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.563
including Google Cloud.

00:01:32.563 --> 00:01:33.980
Let me give you
an example of some

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.860
of the benefits of
infrastructure as code.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.190
This gcloud command will create
a Cloud Storage bucket for you.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.670
But if you run it again,
you'll get an error

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.980
that the bucket already exists.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.190
You could add manual checks
around this command to ask

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.070
if the bucket already exists
and create it if it doesn't.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.250
But when you start adding these
checks around all your scripts,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.613
it gets complex
and unmaintainable.

00:01:58.613 --> 00:02:00.030
MARTIN OMANDER:
So with Terraform,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.280
I won't have to write
so much shell code?

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.450
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Exactly.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.895
In Terraform, you describe
your desired state--

00:02:05.895 --> 00:02:08.669
a bucket exists in your project.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:10.500
Applying the Terraform
manifest will

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.930
check if the desired
state has been met.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.590
If the bucket already exists,
Terraform will take no action.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.940
If the bucket doesn't
exist, Terraform

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.633
will take the steps
required to create it.

00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:24.050
MARTIN OMANDER:
But what if I have

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.750
things that are different
between deployments,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.300
like Google Cloud project ID?

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.800
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: You write
Terraform manifests in HCL--

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.920
HashiCorp Configuration
Language--

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.780
which allows for
such complexities,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.050
like variables and
calculated fields.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.690
Terraform will also work
out the dependency graph

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.240
itself when working
with multiple resources.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.360
Some resources have to
be created before others,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.480
and some resources
create data that will

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.140
be used by other resources.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.530
For example, if you have
a Cloud Run service that

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.480
relies on a Cloud
Storage bucket,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.790
the bucket has to exist
first and Terraform

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.320
will work that out.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.050
If you have, say, a
deployment of five Cloud Run

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.330
Functions that are
independent of each other,

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.070
Terraform will run
those creations

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.010
in parallel, which will be
much faster than creating

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.842
each of those one by one.

00:03:12.842 --> 00:03:15.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Can I configure
other Google Cloud products

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.140
with Terraform?

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.307
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Of course.

00:03:17.307 --> 00:03:19.260
It isn't just limited
to serverless products.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.060
With the Google
Terraform provider,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.670
you can deploy virtual
machines, networking,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.440
and other complex
infrastructure that

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.860
would be downright annoying and
frustrating to have to manually

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.870
deploy over and over again.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, nice.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.500
So could Terraform also
help me with setting up

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.688
test environments?

00:03:37.688 --> 00:03:38.980
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I mean, sure.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.110
Just consider what needs
to be different in test.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.470
You may want to add some
variables to, say, create

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.630
a smaller Cloud SQL
instance, rather

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.460
than a production spec one.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.640
But with Terraform, you can
easily create duplicate setups,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.270
much like a cookie cutter
in your baking adventures.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.890
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds great.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.110
Can I also tell Terraform
to build my containers?

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.530
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well,
Terraform deploys existing

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.080
assets, like containers
or compiled code.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.530
It presumes these
resources already exist.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.260
There are a couple of ways
that you can get around this.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.080
You could have your existing
artifact creation process

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.019
and then manually run
Terraform when required.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.089
That works well
if, like you said,

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:22.370
you deliver your
code to a customer.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.700
Or you can add Terraform to
your existing automation,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.880
for example, as a step in your
Cloud Build configuration.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.310
This works well if you're
doing in-house development

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.230
with continuous deployments.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.420
MARTIN OMANDER: So I should
convert the long list

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.670
of installation instructions
in my readme file

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.527
to a Terraform manifest?

00:04:39.527 --> 00:04:41.360
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: It's
probably a good idea.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.040
Not only will your
deployments get more reliable,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.710
you can store your
live infrastructure

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.910
configuration settings
along with your code

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:48.830
in source control.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.047
I've left some links in
the description for you.

00:04:51.047 --> 00:04:52.130
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.960
I will read up on that.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.360
So could you show me an
example of a Terraform

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.477
manifest for a serverless
web application?

00:04:58.477 --> 00:05:00.560
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: How about
we catch up next week,

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.870
and sit down and work
with some Terraform code,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.278
and try deploying a few things?

00:05:04.278 --> 00:05:06.320
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, that
would give me a better

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.000
idea of how Terraform works.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.200
Let's do it.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.200
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Great.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

